To create a Rust application that supports the Eleven Labs API and reads the contents of the clipboard aloud using Text-to-Speech (TTS), you can follow these steps:

1. Install Rust:
   Follow the instructions on the official Rust website to install Rust: https://www.rust-lang.org/tools/install

2. Create a new Rust project:
   Open your terminal and run the following command to create a new Rust project:

   ```
   cargo new elevenlabs_tts --bin
   ```

3. Add dependencies:
   In the `Cargo.toml` file, add the following dependencies:

   ```toml
   [dependencies]
   reqwest = { version = "0.11", features = ["json"] }
   serde = { version = "1.0", features = ["derive"] }
   serde_json = "1.0"
   clipboard = "3.0"
   tokio = { version = "1.17", features = ["full"] }
   ```

   This will add the required dependencies for making HTTP requests, parsing JSON, handling the clipboard, and using the Tokio runtime for asynchronous operations.

4. Implement the main function:
   Open the `src/main.rs` file and replace its content with the following code:

   ```rust
   use reqwest::Client;
   use serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};
   use clipboard::ClipboardProvider;
   use std::env;
   use tokio::runtime::Runtime;

   const API_KEY: &str = "YOUR_API_KEY";

   #[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]
   struct RequestBody {
       text: String,
   }

   #[tokio::main]
   async fn main() {
       let clipboard = ClipboardProvider::new().unwrap();
       let clipboard_content = clipboard.get_contents().unwrap();

       let client = Client::new();
       let runtime = Runtime::new().unwrap();
       let request_body = RequestBody { text: clipboard_content };
       let url = "https://api.elevenlabs.io/v1/text-to-speech";

       let response = runtime.block_on(async {
           client
               .post(url)
               .header("Authorization", format!("Bearer {}", API_KEY))
               .json(&request_body)
               .send()
               .await
               .unwrap()
               .json::<serde_json::Value>()
               .await
               .unwrap()
       });

       let audio_url = response["audio_url"].as_str().unwrap();
       println!("Audio URL: {}", audio_url);

       // TODO: Implement playing the audio URL
   }
   ```

   Replace `YOUR_API_KEY` with your Eleven Labs API key.

5. Run the application:
   In the terminal, navigate to the project directory and run the following command:

   ```
   cargo run
   ```

   This will compile and run the application. The audio URL will be printed in the console.

6. Play the audio URL:
   To play the audio URL, you can use a library like `wasm-bindgen-media-api` for web assembly or `rodio` for native Rust. Implement the TODO comment in the code above to play the audio URL.

7. (Optional) Package the application:
   If you want to package the application for distribution, you can use tools like `cargo-deb` for Debian-based systems or `cargo-x` for cross-platform builds. Follow the instructions on their respective websites to set up and use these tools.

